[Music]  hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar using cutting edge stem cell  technology to model human cardiac  diseases i am sydney mcneil of lab roots  and i'll be your moderator for today's  event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by lab roots and brought to  you by miltoni biotech  to learn more visit miltoniubiotech.com  we encourage you to participate today by  submitting any questions you may have  during the presentation  to do so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click send  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for at the end of the  presentation  you may also submit any technical issues  here as well if you have trouble seeing  or hearing the presentation  we only have ariane with us for 30  minutes today  any questions that we are unable to  answer will be answered by our speaker  via the email address you provided at  the time of registration  this webinar is educational and thus  offers free continuing education credits  please click on the continuing education  window at the bottom of your screen to  obtain your credits  i would like to now welcome our speaker  post-doctoral fellow dr ariane caudle  from the laboratory of dr joseph wu  in the cardiovascular institute at  stanford university ariane you may now  begin your presentation  well thank you very much for that very  kind introduction  i'm truly honored and excited to be here  so thank you very much for the  invitation  today my talk is entitled  using cutting edge stem cell technology  to model human cardiac diseases so let's  get started  for today's presentation i'm very  excited to share our group's  contributions to the exciting field of  cardiac diseases and induced pluripotent  stem cell or ipsc technology  i'll first introduce some central themes  with some relevant background and then  i'll spend a lot of time in the  following three main sections  first how we generate and maintain ipsc  lines  next how we can differentiate these  lines into cardiac cell types and then  finally how we can use these models as  viable tools to study disease  with specific emphasis on some exciting  therapeutic targets that our lab has  elucidated in the past few years  finally i'll summarize what we have  learned today and discuss some possible  directions for the future of this  technology  so cardiovascular disease  which is broadly defined as  pathologies of the heart blood vessels  and the vascular system of the brain  accounts for nearly one-third of total  deaths worldwide  there are various etiologies of  cardiovascular disease  that exist and here i've highlighted  just a few of them  but for today's talk we'll focus on just  one specific type of cardiovascular  disease  which is the dilated cardiomyopathies  for the rest of the talk this will be  abbreviated as dcm  so with a prevalence of at least one in  every 2 500 people dcm is the most  common cause of heart failure after  coronary artery disease  as well as the leading indication for  heart transplant  the cost of managing dcm in the u.s  alone has been estimated to be between 4  and 10 billion annually  gcm is characterized by ventricular  chamber dilation or enlargement  and thinning of the heart wall as  depicted by this schematic  highlighting just the left ventricles  dcm can be clearly observed using  ultrasound  as i'm showing here from patients  and furthermore histological analysis of  patient cardiac tissues reveals very  striking changes at the cellular level  between healthy and dcm patients  part of the difficulty with treating dcm  is that it has really broad molecular  manifestations  so mutations in genes encoding  sarcomeric  cytoskeletal  mitochondrial  nuclear membrane and even calcium  metabolism proteins account for nearly  50 percent of dcm cases  and although there are at least 33 genes  associated to dates  over 75 percent of the known cases have  mutations residing in just a handful of  genes some of which i've highlighted in  this table  in today's talk i'll further highlight  two key genes  lamin a  which structurally supports the cell's  nuclear envelope  and the cardiac troponin t which is part  of the contractile machinery of the  heart that helps it beat  there is a clear need for new therapies  and cutting-edge therapeutics for  cardiovascular diseases  however there are still several barriers  to research  so first the cardiomyocytes are  post-mitotic  meaning unlike many cell types they will  not divide in a dish so we can't just  continuously grow them in the lab  so patient samples are therefore very  limited  and  healthy controls which we need for  comparison are even more difficult to  obtain  so historically animal models have been  used  but they remain a low throughput and  very expensive option and it's not  compatible with with high throughput  drug screening  furthermore small animal models don't  fully recapitulate human cardiac  physiology  and there are inherent differences in  heart rate and disease progression  our lab utilizes the induced pluripotent  stem cell or ipsc platform to model  cardiac diseases from humans  this model has many key advantages  the first is that stem cells can be  differentiated into a variety of  specialized cells  some of which we can recapitulate in a  lab  including our favorites the cardiac cell  types  and therefore stem cells can provide a  limitless material for research  in our case this circumvents the  post-mitotic nature of the adult  cardiomyocyte  second they genetically match the people  that they are derived from  meaning that we can study many different  types of heart diseases across a wide  range of demographics and populations  and that sample is more relevant to  designing therapies  third the cell-based format is very  compatible with large-scale screening of  different drugs  gene therapies and intervention  strategies which we can then directly  test the effects of  with cell-based functional readouts  so how exactly do we do this  here is a general overview of our ipsc  pipeline  we start with somatic cells from  patients  usually in the form of blood or skin  fibroblasts because those are easy to  extract  and we reprogram them into ipscs  then we differentiate into our cell type  of interest  and then we use combinatorial strategies  to determine disease mechanisms  then try to interrogate and rescue them  our end goal is to find viable treatment  strategies that are translational to  clinical applications  so first let's dive in detail into what  reprogramming actually is  and how we generate validate and  characterize ipsc lines  i thought i would share our most recent  publication from the lab led by  postdoctoral fellow dr sancho  published in january in stem cell  research  here our lab generated three  heterozygous laminae mutation carrying  ipsc lines from three cardiac  laminopathy patients  laminae is a rod-shaped structural  scaffold of the nucleus  it forms these long tetrameric bundles  that functionally supports  and organize chromatin and therefore  playing an important role in dna  replication and signal transduction  mutations in laminae  called laminopathies  largely result in cardiac diseases such  as dcm  but the underlying mechanisms still  remain unclear  therefore having models for  laminopathies where we can test drugs  are incredibly valuable resources  so from three dcm patients two male and  one female blood was collected  peripheral blood mononuclear cells or  pbmcs  were isolated from the blood using  separation techniques  and then stabilized in media and  supplements  next we use vectors based on a modified  non-transmissible form of sending virus  to safely and effectively deliver and  express key genetic factors  necessary for reprogramming somatic  cells into ipscs  and then clonally select for cells that  were transduced  once we have single colonies we let the  now ips cells recover and propagate  and typically the cell culture media of  ipscs needs to be replaced every day  however we recently switched to using  stem max ips brew media which only  requires a media change every two days  which just greatly improves the  day-to-day practicality of using ipsc  models  and as you can see the cells are both  happy and healthy  one of the first things we validate is  cell morphology and genotype so here you  can see the very quintessential  stem cell morphology is present in all  three patient lines  these patients have a mutation in the  lamin a gene  which changes uh guanine to adenine to  single base permutation that essentially  changes  arginine 133 to glutamine and this amino  acid resides in the central raw domain  of the laminae protein  we are able to validate the mutation is  present in all three lines using sanger  sequencing  next we look at the whole genome array  and validate that each ipsc cell line  has a normal karyotype  and then we look by quantitative rtpcr  that the sendai virus that we use for  reprogramming  which is non-integrating is no longer  present in the cell  here we use an early passage ipsc line  as a positive control  and a late passage ipsc line as a  negative control and as you can see at  late passages all three cell lines  express very low to no sev transcript  next we use immunostaining to look for  the key pluripotency markers nanog sox2  and ox34  as you can see these cells express all  three markers which all have high  detectable transcript levels  finally we validate each cell line's  differentiation potential  so stem cells should have the capacity  to become all three germ layers right  the outer ectodermine or the outer  ectoderm  which essentially becomes uh skin cells  pigment cells and neurons the middle  mesoderm  which for us is very important right  because that yields all of the muscle  and the inner endoderm  so to confirm we stand for derm layer  specific markers  and then show  that these lines can therefore  differentiate into all three  germ layers and there have been  successfully reprogrammed  once we have functional ipsc lines we  then differentiate them into cardiac  cell types  for today's talk i'll focus mostly on  ips derived cardiomyocytes  ipscs first become committed mesoderm  before becoming cardiac progenitors and  then early cardiomyocytes  and as early cardiomyocytes they will  begin to physically beat in a dish you  can very easily see this by light  microscope  and then further maturation of the  cardiomyocytes  just with prolonged culture  or other genetic or molecular  interventions increasingly starts to  resemble the transcriptional profile of  mammalian models  logistically this takes about 30 days in  the lab to get from ipsc to  cardiomyocyte  starting from the ips brew this requires  several media changes  alongside interrogation of wind  signaling  to yield committed mesoderm  then addition of insulin when the cells  start beating  and then two days of glucose-free  lactate selection to increase purity  using this strategy we can generate  patient-matched ipsc-derived  cardiomyocytes  to exemplify this work i'll briefly  describe this paper from our lab  led by dr ning sun and published in 2012  in science translational medicine  where  patient derived ipsc lines  were used to model dilated  cardiomyopathies  here we generated cardiomyocytes from  ipscs derived from patients in a dcm  family  carrying a point mutation in the gene  encoding the sarcomeric protein cardiac  troponin t  cardiac troponin t plays a role in the  contractile machinery of the heart  and compared to control healthy  individuals  cardiomyocytes derived from dcm patients  exhibited abnormal distribution of  sarcomeric alpha actinin  i'm shown here in red by immunostaining  and as you can see in the merge there  are these tiny pancta in the dcm cells  that are not present in the control  they also have abnormal calcium ion  regulation  detected here with representative line  scan images and decreased spontaneous  calcium transients  and therefore and furthermore if you can  take these cells and make embroidered  bodies  which are three-dimensional aggregates  of cells and stress them with norin or  norepinephrine  the dcm embryo bodies have a very poor  response and greatly diminished beating  rate  followed by sarcomeric disarray and cell  disorganization as you can see from the  immunostaining  to summarize the cells demonstrated in a  dish  common phenotypes of  observed in dcm patients  so  how can we further use this model to  gain new therapeutic insight  so at the time circa  2a gene therapy  and  metametoprolol  both had positive effects in dcm trials  but the underlying mechanism for why  they worked was still unresolved  hence our lab set out to investigate if  these mechanisms of dcm repair could be  recapitulated in a dish  so alongside improvements in gene  expression in dcm  the circa  to a  over expression improved contraction  force  as measured by atomic force microscopy  in parallel they found that metoprolol  treatment for one week significantly  decreased the number of single dcm  cardiomyocytes with disorganized  sarcomeres  these results suggest that blockade  of the beta-adrenergic pathway help dcm  ipsc derive cardiomyocytes to resist  mechanical  deterioration  and improve their myofilament  organization  to further explore beta adrenergic  signaling in ipsc cardiomyocytes and its  role in dcm  authors from our lab tried using beta  agonists so this study was led by dr  howdy wu  and was published in cell stem cell in  2015.  here they found that the beta-adrenergic  agonist isoproterenol  induced mature beta-adrenergic signaling  becomes compromised in disease  in order to test the effects of  beta-adrenergic stimulation  ipsc cardiomyocytes were next treated  with isoproterenol and spontaneous  calcium transients were analyzed  isoproterenol  increases cardiac contractility so while  the control cells respond to  isoproterenol challenge  the dcm cells do not  although expression levels of several  beta adrenergic signaling components  were unaltered between the control and  dcm ipsc cardiomyocytes they actually  found that the phosphodiesterases  pde2a and 3a were upregulated in dcm  cardiomyocytes  so the phosphodiesterase is essentially  cleave cyclic amp and this is a very  important process  in a lot of different cell pathways  but notably the pharmacologic inhibition  of the pde2a  and pde3a restored cyclic amp levels and  actually immediately ameliorated the  impaired vaginertic signaling of dcm  cardiomyocytes suggesting that  the therapeutic potential of of  targeting these pdes  so these studies um especially this one  really shows  you know  what kind what pathways are important in  the normal cell and in the disease cell  but importantly also presents a solution  and that's essentially what our lab  looks for in  in um  in interesting projects and  collaborations  in summary i showed you how our group is  using patient derived cells for  reprogramming of ipscs  we discussed how cardiomyocytes can be  differentiated and then i showed you two  examples  of how these models have been employed  to gain a deeper understanding of dcm  pathogenesis  to conclude  ipsc technology offers an exceptional  opportunity for creating disease  specific cellular models  investigating underlying mechanisms and  optimizing therapy  in the future we hope to really come  full circle to continue to integrate  other cardiac cell types into these  systems so today i talked about  cardiomyocytes we can also make cardiac  endothelial cells and fibroblasts we can  put them together in really interesting  combinations and stoichiometries  to really fully recapitulate that  complex cellular  microenvironment  and by doing that we get closer to  disease  therapies that are  eventually going to be more  applicable to translational applications  so we want to come full circle and we  want to generate novel therapies and  models that can actually help real world  patients  um and and to really to do that in a  manner that is personalized to that  patient and what they need  i'd like to thank my lab and my mentor  dr josephu  the stanford biobank  and cvi is listed here  and my funding sources so thank you  everyone for your attention  um i'm very happy to take any comments  or questions  um yeah thank you  all right thank you arianne for your  informative presentation we will now  start the live q a portion of the  webinar if you have a question you would  like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on your screen we will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for  let's get started our first question is  our ipsc derived  cardiomyocytes exactly like adult  cardiomyocytes what are some limitations  to the model  that's a great question  so they are not exactly like adult  cardiomyocytes comparatively they are  still quite immature in  morphology metabolism and some of the  ion channels  however there has been a lot of work  from a lot of different labs  for strategies to improve the maturation  phenotypes  so a lot of people are either using  genetic strategies  changing metabolism which means like  changing the media how much glucose to  give how much fatty acids to give  people are trying mechanical force  electrical stress  lots of different options and a lot of  them are are very promising i think to  date kind of a  combined approach  is the gold standard but there are  definitely  ways to improve the model so it's a  great question  great thank you our next question is  can you put different types of ipsc  derived self together to stimulate the  cardiac environment  yes great question absolutely um i tried  to kind of allude to this allude to this  uh this is something that i find really  fascinating so you can put cells  together you can put them as a so-called  co-culture so in a monolayer just in a  dish you can put cells together and see  how they interact with each other  physically  you can also kind of put them on  different platforms so they're not  physically touching but  that's kind of a way to look at what  signals are secreted between them  this allows you to kind of detect  self-cell communications  which is a clue to how you know disease  propagates across the whole organ so  that's actually a very important facet  of our research  the other thing you can do is to grow  different cell types as  three-dimensional aggregates  they're called engineered heart tissues  ehts  you essentially take millions of cells  and you grow them in a mold  around two posts they kind of engraft  and you form a tissue  that beats as one unit it's very cool  and it's it's much more  relevant than some of the monolayer  models so yes you can and it's a very  exciting and really beautiful part  if you look in some of our publications  you can see  what these look like and i'd highly  recommend it  perfect thank you our next question is  besides genetic models what other types  of stress can ipsc-based models be used  to study  oh okay um yes so in the example shown  today i mostly show genetic models or  stress induced by drugs but actually  we're interested in lots of different  types of stress so we're looking at  kind of environmental stress  we're even looking at radiation exposure  hypoxia  changes in diet et cetera  um those are all things we can certainly  model on a cell-based platform  personally i'm also interested in in  physiological stress  such as exercise and pregnancy and  things like that um those are a little  bit more difficult to model  uh in in cell culture but i hope to soon  get there thanks for your question  thank you our last question is you  mentioned using patient-derived cells  but can ipscs be generated from healthy  people too  yes yes so this is a very interesting  question  um we typically look at patients just  because we want to find  therapies  but but i actually think that you also  need to look at healthy people too right  and so  um from the healthy people we're we're  looking  mostly across different ethnic groups  gender you know things that can really  affect the genetic background and then  and then trying to kind of tease out  how that manifests into cellular  phenotypes  it's very difficult to do that but i  think it's really an important platform  to understand the cohesive model  so that's definitely of high interest  here thank you for your question  thank you again arianne for your time  today do you have any final remarks  yeah thank you for having me i i just  want to kind of advocate for our work  if you find any part of this  presentation  exciting at all please reach out  email me with your questions  i'll get back to you as soon as i can  and also know that our lab is very open  to collaboration  and sharing any of these models  so please check out our lab websites  the wu lab at stanford cardiovascular  institute  and reach out if  if you want to work with us we're very  excited thank you  thank you again arianne for your time  today and your important research we  would also like to thank labruz and our  sponsor miltenu biotech for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time  goodbye  you

